<h1>Fernando Mendoza <em>Combine Profile</em></h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📅 Updated August 23, 2026</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏈 Las Vegas Raiders</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">📊 Measurements Only — No Drills</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Fernando Mendoza is the first No. 1 overall pick since 2007 to go straight to the Raiders — and one of the rare top picks who never ran a single combine drill. He measured in at 6'4.75" and 236 pounds, drew immediate Josh Allen comparisons, then skipped the 40, the vertical, everything. His pro day was throwing-only. His first real athletic evidence came Week 1 of the preseason. Here's the full measurement profile and how it held up against a live NFL pass rush.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">
<h2>The Measurements — Combine, Not Drills</h2>
<div class="player-card">
<div class="player-name">Fernando Mendoza</div>
<div class="player-sub">Las Vegas Raiders · Quarterback · No. 1 Overall Pick, 2026</div>
<div class="player-stats">
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">6'4.75"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Height — QB1 territory</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">236 lbs</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Combine Weight</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">31 7/8"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Arm Length</div>
</div>
<div class="player-stat above">
<div class="player-stat-val">9.5"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Hand Size</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Mendoza checked in at the 2026 NFL Scouting Combine at 6-foot-4¾ and 236 pounds, with a 9½-inch hand and a 31⅞-inch arm — measurements that produced a 76¾-inch wingspan, nearly identical to Josh Allen's 77 inches as a prospect. The comparisons wrote themselves: Allen entered the league at 6'5", 237 pounds, a near carbon copy of Mendoza's frame. Analysts were also quick to note his hands are the same size as or larger than Tom Brady's, Lamar Jackson's, and Sam Darnold's — well within normal range for the position, despite some early social-media chatter calling them small.</p>
<p>What Mendoza didn't do at the combine is just as notable as what he did. He skipped every on-field drill — no 40-yard dash, no vertical, no broad jump, no throwing session in Indianapolis. His agents made the calculation that a presumptive No. 1 pick has far more to lose than gain from a bad rep or a slow split. He carried that same approach into his pro day on April 1, where he ran no timed drills at all — only a throwing session, completing 53 of 56 attempts, including his first 23 in a row.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🏈</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Why skip the drills entirely:</strong> Mendoza was already the presumptive No. 1 pick heading into combine week off a Heisman-winning, 16-0 national championship season at Indiana. For a prospect in that position, every rep at the combine is pure downside — a bad time doesn't move him up, but a bad one could genuinely cost millions. Standing on measurements alone is an increasingly common strategy for consensus top picks, and Mendoza followed it to the letter.</div>
</div>
