<div class="rank-row">
<div class="rank-label"><strong>Hand Size</strong>9.5"</div>
<div class="rank-track"><div class="rank-fill" style="width:62%;background:#00cfff"></div></div>
<div class="rank-val">Normal range</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The Josh Allen comparison is the one that matters most for Raiders fans, since Allen's combine profile is one of the clearest recent examples of a big, mobile quarterback whose measurements translated directly into professional durability and off-platform playmaking. Mendoza is an eighth of an inch shorter and a pound lighter than Allen was as a prospect — a difference so small it's effectively the same build. Whether that translates to the same level of NFL production is an entirely separate question the measurements can't answer.</p>
<h2>The Preseason Debut — First Real Data Point</h2>
<p>With no combine drills and a throwing-only pro day, Mendoza's first genuine athletic test as a professional came in his preseason debut against the Arizona Cardinals in mid-August. Playing 31 snaps behind veteran Kirk Cousins, who continues to run the starting offense, Mendoza completed 10 of 16 passes for 97 yards and a touchdown, adding two carries for 6 yards on the ground.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Stat</th>
<th>Preseason Debut vs Cardinals</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Completions</td><td>10-of-16 (62.5%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Passing Yards</td><td>97</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touchdowns</td><td>1 (to Jack Bech)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rushing</td><td>2 carries, 6 yards</td></tr>
<tr><td>PFF Overall Grade</td><td>66.7</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sacks Taken</td><td>1</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>His touchdown throw was a tight-window laser to Jack Bech on third-and-goal, and his best individual play of the night came off play-action — a 19-yard strike between three defenders. Head coach Klint Kubiak called him "an ascending player" postgame, noting he ran the huddle and handled play calls the same way he had in practice. It wasn't a flawless outing; Mendoza acknowledged some throws lacked the timing and anticipation Cousins shows consistently, an area he said he still needs to develop.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">📈</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>What the debut actually tells you:</strong> One preseason game against backups and rotational defenders is a small sample, and PFF's own analysis noted the Raiders didn't dress up the offense with much play-action or screens for him. But it's still the first data point of Mendoza translating his measured size into a live pocket — pressure recognition, footwork under duress, accuracy on the move — none of which a tape measure or a scale can predict.</div>
</div>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What are Fernando Mendoza's combine measurements?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Mendoza measured 6-foot-4¾ (sometimes rounded to 6'5") and 236 pounds at the 2026 NFL Scouting Combine, with a 9½-inch hand size and a 31⅞-inch arm length, producing a wingspan of roughly 76¾ inches.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Did Fernando Mendoza run the 40-yard dash?</div>
<div class="faq-a">No. Mendoza skipped every on-field drill at the combine, including the 40-yard dash, vertical jump, and broad jump. He also ran no timed drills at his pro day, opting for a throwing-only session instead.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How does Fernando Mendoza compare to Josh Allen?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Mendoza's combine measurements (6'4.75", 236 lbs) are nearly identical to Josh Allen's as a prospect (6'5", 237 lbs) — a difference of about an eighth of an inch and one pound. The physical comparison is close; how their careers compare is a separate, still-unanswered question.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Is Fernando Mendoza starting for the Raiders?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Not yet. Kirk Cousins is running the starting offense to begin the 2026 season, with the Raiders planning a gradual transition for their rookie No. 1 overall pick rather than starting him immediately.</div>
